course: language
lesson: 14
title: Punctuation that changes meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Punctuation marks are the traffic signs of writing: they tell the reader where to stop, pause, ask or shout, and moving one can change the whole meaning.
explain: The famous one: "Let's eat Grandma!" makes Grandma the dinner, while "Let's eat, Grandma!" invites her to the table; the comma before a name you're talking to saves lives.
explain: "A panda eats shoots and leaves" means it eats bamboo shoots and leaves; add commas, "eats, shoots and leaves", and the panda has lunch, fires a gun and walks out; that joke is the title of a famous British book about punctuation.
explain: Pairs of commas decide who said what: "The teacher said the pupil is a fool" and "The teacher, said the pupil, is a fool" swap who gets insulted.
explain: Texts have their own feel: a lone "fine." can look cold, "fine!!" looks cheerful, while in school work one exclamation mark is plenty and a question mark needs no full stop after it.
fact: A comma shows a short pause or separates parts of a sentence; a full stop ends a sentence.
fact: A comma goes before or after the name of a person you are talking to: "Let's eat, Grandma!"
fact: "Eats, Shoots and Leaves" is the title of a famous British book about punctuation; shoots are young plant stems.
fact: A run-on sentence joins two or more complete sentences with no proper punctuation; fix it with full stops or joining words.
fact: A long sentence is not a run-on if it is punctuated properly.
fact: A pair of commas can put extra words to one side and change who is speaking: "The teacher, said the pupil, is a fool."
fact: A colon (:) introduces something; with "Woman: without her, man is nothing" the same words as "Woman without her man is nothing" mean the opposite.
fact: In texting, a full stop after a short word can look cold or annoyed, especially to younger people; one study found texts ending with a full stop seemed less friendly.
fact: In formal writing one exclamation mark is enough, and only when it's really needed.
fact: A question mark or exclamation mark already ends a sentence, so no full stop goes after it.
fact: "My brother who lives in Leeds is a doctor" (no commas) picks out one of several brothers; "My brother, who lives in Leeds, is a doctor" (commas) just adds a fact about your only brother.
fact: Signs need punctuation too: "Coffee and cake £3.50. Vegans welcome!"
fact: A dash marks a sudden break or adds extra information, like brackets; a hyphen is shorter and joins words, as in well-known.
fact: In the North East of England "pet" is a friendly word, like dear or love.
exercise: Which sentence invites Grandma to eat with you? | Let's eat, Grandma! | Let's eat Grandma! | Lets eat Grandma! | Let's, eat Grandma!
exercise: What job does the comma do in "Let's eat, Grandma!"? | It shows you're talking to Grandma | It shows that Grandma is the food for dinner | It shows a list of foods | It ends the sentence
exercise: "A panda eats shoots and leaves." What does it eat? | Young plant stems and leaves | A gun, and then some leaves after that | Nothing, it runs away | Only bamboo leaves
exercise: What does "A panda eats, shoots and leaves" say? | It eats, fires a gun, then goes away | It eats young bamboo shoots and leaves | It eats leaves and grows shoots | It leaves its food behind
exercise: Daniel writes "I like cake I like ice cream I like jelly." What is it? | A run-on sentence | A perfect sentence | An exclamation | A question
exercise: What's a good fix? | I like cake, ice cream and jelly. | I like cake I, like ice cream I like, jelly. | I like. Cake I like ice. Cream I like jelly. | I like cake! I like! ice cream jelly
exercise: Is every long sentence a run-on? | No, long sentences are fine if properly punctuated | Yes, all long sentences are wrong, however they are punctuated | Yes, if it has more than ten words | No, run-ons are short sentences
exercise: In "The teacher, said the pupil, is a fool", who is called a fool? | The teacher | The pupil | Nobody | Both of them
exercise: "Woman: without her, man is nothing." Who needs whom? | Man needs woman | Woman needs man | Nobody needs anyone | Both are nothing
exercise: Ellie's mate texts "fine." What might it feel like? | Cold or annoyed | Very excited | Confused | Sleepy
exercise: Sanna writes "Hello!!!!!!!!!" on a card. Her Geordie grandad writes "One is enough, pet." What does he mean? | One exclamation mark is enough, dear | Write hello only once, and give the card to the pet dog | Buy one card, not ten | One pet is enough for the family
exercise: Lucas writes "Can I come?." What's wrong? | A full stop after a question mark | Nothing at all, it is perfectly correct | It needs two question marks | It needs a comma after I
exercise: Ava has three brothers. Which is right? | My brother who lives in Leeds is a doctor. | My brother, who lives in Leeds, is a doctor. | My, brother who lives in Leeds is a doctor. | My brother who lives, in Leeds is a doctor.
exercise: A café sign says "Coffee and cake £3.50 vegans welcome!" How should it read? | Coffee and cake £3.50. Vegans welcome! | Coffee and cake. £3.50 vegans welcome! | Coffee, and cake £3.50 vegans, welcome! | Coffee and cake £3.50 vegans. Welcome!
exercise: What does a dash do? | Marks a sudden break or adds extra information | Joins two words together, like well-known or ice-cold | Ends a question | Shows something belongs to someone
exercise: Which needs a hyphen, not a dash? | a well-known singer | there it was - a spider! | I opened the door - and screamed | the cat - a huge one - sat
exercise: How many full stops are needed to fix "it rained we stayed in we played chess" into three sentences? | Three | One | Two | Four
test: Your mate says "commas are just for breathing, they don't change nothing." What's the best reply? | "Let's eat Grandma" and "Let's eat, Grandma" prove they change meaning | He's right, commas are just decoration for breathing in | Commas only matter in poems | Commas change meaning only in French
test: A Gen Z sister texts "k." after you ask to borrow her charger. What's probably going on? | The full stop makes it look cold, so she may be annoyed | She is really happy to lend it to you straight away, no problem | She has typed the letter k by accident and is fine | It means "kindly come"
test: What would happen if a recipe had no punctuation? "Add salt pepper the onion stir" | You might not know where steps end or what is listed | Nothing, because recipes never need any punctuation at all | The cake would bake faster | It would become a poem
test: A Scouse lad writes "Our kid, who's sound, fixed me bike." ("Our kid" means my brother, "sound" means nice.) What do the commas show? | Extra information about his brother | That he has many brothers to choose from | A list of three people | A question
test: Your little brother writes "Help me, Tom!" and "Help me Tom!" in two stories. In which one is he talking to Tom? | Help me, Tom! | Help me Tom! | Both equally | Neither
test: A boomer neighbour's sign says "Slow children at play." What makes it funny, and what's the fix? | It sounds like the children are slow; "Slow: children at play" | Children never play near roads | The sign should say "Fast children" | Nothing is funny about it
test: In a cartoon, a teacher writes "Stop clapping!!!!!!!!" on a report. What would a formal report use? | One exclamation mark, or none | Ten exclamation marks | A full stop after each one | A question mark and an exclamation mark
test: Your cousin writes "What time is it?." Which is right? | What time is it? | What time is it?. | What time is it.? | What time is it!.
test: "I find inspiration in cooking my family and my dog." What is the fix? | I find inspiration in cooking, my family and my dog. | I find inspiration, in cooking my family and my dog. | I find inspiration in cooking my family, and my dog. | I find, inspiration in cooking my family and my dog.
test: A London lad texts "bare jokes!!!" ("Bare" means very, lots of.) Is three exclamation marks OK there? | Yes, in a casual text it shows fun | No, texts can never have exclamation marks | No, it must be a question mark | Yes, and also in a formal essay
test: Puzzle: add commas to "Mum said Dad is late" so that Dad is the speaker and Mum is the one who is late. | Mum, said Dad, is late | Mum said, Dad is late | Mum said Dad, is late | Mum said Dad is, late
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the comma in the sentence "Let's eat, Grandma!"? | It marks the direct address of the person named | It separates items in a list of foods | It concludes the sentence | It indicates possession by the person named
exam: Identify the principal defect of the sentence "I like cake I like ice cream I like jelly". | It joins complete sentences without appropriate punctuation | It contains no verb and therefore cannot form a complete sentence | It is excessively brief | It employs an incorrect tense
exam: Select the response consistent with the established conventions concerning the sentence "Can I come?.". | The full stop is superfluous, the question mark already concluding the sentence | A further question mark is required | The question mark ought to be replaced by a comma | The sentence is correctly punctuated
exam: In respect of the sentence "The teacher, said the pupil, is a fool", ascertain the person to whom the epithet "fool" is applied. | The teacher | The pupil | Neither person | Both persons
